WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.350
The policy plane isn't one box.

2
00:00:02.350 --> 00:00:07.442
It has three parts: an on-premises DNS
and DHCP layer that replaces AD DNS,

3
00:00:07.442 --> 00:00:10.895
a management plane holding
the system of record,

4
00:00:10.895 --> 00:00:15.987
and local resolvers that keep
answering if management is unreachable.

5
00:00:15.987 --> 00:00:19.363
It overlays the cloud services;
it doesn't replace them.

6
00:00:19.363 --> 00:00:22.377
Changes converge in
seconds, not milliseconds,

7
00:00:22.377 --> 00:00:26.551
so an emergency block has to be
verified where clients are served.

8
00:00:26.551 --> 00:00:28.383
Infoblox is the book's example,

9
00:00:28.383 --> 00:00:32.050
and its government cloud offering
is certified at Moderate.

10
00:00:32.050 --> 00:00:35.269
The architecture stays the
same in federal clouds;

11
00:00:35.269 --> 00:00:37.875
authorization and integrations change.

12
00:00:37.875 --> 00:00:42.168
A Moderate plane doesn't cover High
systems, so use a service authorized

13
00:00:42.168 --> 00:00:46.025
at the right level, or keep the
plane inside the agency's boundary.

14
00:00:46.025 --> 00:00:49.728
Government regions such as GovCloud
host Moderate systems too,

15
00:00:49.728 --> 00:00:53.012
so their gaps matter at Moderate,
and the slide lists them.

16
00:00:53.012 --> 00:00:56.470
The right-hand column is High
only, kept for reference.
